'''Ecological Action''' ({{lang-bl|Acción ecolóxica}}), referred to as the '''Ecologists''' ({{Lang-bl|ecolóxistas}}) in the [[Confederal Congress]], or the '''Agrarians''' ({{lang-bl|Agrarios}}) in the media, is a {{W|platformism|federation}} of {{W|social ecology (Bookchin)|social ecologist}} organizations within the [[Balisca|Baliscan Confederation]]. It is representative of a variety political movements in Balisca, including multiple left-wing, autonomous, militant groups and individuals who describe themselves as resisting and reversing {{W|environmental degradation}} while striving for international {{W|climate justice}} and an {{W|ecological civilization}}. Its membership has historically been made up of activists originating from and representing the [[rural society in Balisca|rural]] Baliscan [[communes of Balisca|communes]], but made significant inroads into Baliscan [[Autonomous commune (Balisca)|urban communes]] during the [[2021 Baliscan confederal election|2021 election cycle]].

The group advocates facets of {{W|isolationism}}, and it supports the withdrawal of Balisca from {{W|Intergovernmental organization|intergovernmental}} and {{W|supranational organisations}} that it argues supports {{W|neocolonialism}}, including the [[Adonian Community]] and the {{W|World Trade Organization}}, calls for the revocation of the [[Kuluba Accords]], while simultaneously supporting Balisca's membership of [[COCO]] and it's aspiration for {{W|global justice movement|global justice}}. It also stands for expanding the scope of Balisca's policy of {{W|armed neutrality|neutrality}}. They believe that the [[Confederal Armed Forces|Armed Forces]] should become a fully decentralized {{W|militia force}} and should never become involved in interventions abroad.

== Ideology ==

==== Densification ====
Anti-urbanization within the Agrarian Front has been strongly influenced by the work ''Agràpoli'' by [[Jonatan Pijoan]], first published in 1948. This book, while profusely urbophobic, sparked significant public interest in spatial planning and {{W|urban design}}, and revitalized the movement for {{W|compact city|compact cites}} in Balisca. It recommends extensive methods to further {{W|decentralization|decentralize}} (and eventually fully abolish) the Baliscan state, to reduce the influence of [[Castejón]] and its macrocephalous [[Concordia|conurbation]], and implement the concept of {{W|bioregionalism}}. Since its publishing, ''Agràpoli'' has become to be favored reading material in Baliscan schools, and has become widespread in literary courses.

Progressively, the Baliscan anti-urban vision has changed its goal, turning from simple opposition to urban expansion, to the promotion of {{W|degrowth}}, {{W|compact cities}}, {{W|restoration ecology}} and {{W|rewilding}} to halt and eventually reverse {{W|degradation of the environment}}. 

In Balisca, the politics of the city rest on a libertine and utopian vision of urbanism, and on an enchantment with the city center. For a long time, [[Rural society in Balisca|rural Baliscan society]] has remained preoccupied with a sentiment of hostility to the city stemming from their encroachment on the environment and the altering of the natural ecosystem. The country and the rural society are perceived as holding and conserving "authentic" [[Baliscan values]]—notably with regard to the environment— including {{W|Communalism (Bookchin)|communalism}}, opposition to {{W|hierarchy}}, connection with land, and sense of responsibility to the {{W|common good}}.

To prevent these ills and {{W|suburbanization}}, the Agrarians pioneered {{W|fFree public transport|free and cooperative transit}} and increased {{W|urban density}} to prevent {{W|urban sprawl}} and {{W|car dependency}}, and {{W|active design|active}} and {{W|Healthy city|healthy communities}} to ensure high {{W|quality of life}} and sharing of {{W|economic resources}}. Additionally, it is largely responsible for the lack of lower density {{W|suburb|suburban development}} around Baliscan cities throughout the country. Paradoxically, this influential environmentally-oriented outlook then gave rise to widespread {{W|rural–urban fringe}} and {{W|wildland–urban interface}} across the country.

